"Relates long-term objectives, annual priorities, improvement initiatives, measures and ownership in a compact strategy-deployment view.\n\nUse when: Check whether annual work supports a few breakthrough objectives. Review alignment and ownership across functions.\n\nAvoid when: The organization has not agreed strategic priorities or the relationships become too dense to read.\n\nVisual grammar: Four connected matrix regions organize objectives, priorities, initiatives and measures. Relationship marks indicate meaningful alignment across adjacent regions. An ownership area records responsible leaders under a declared convention.\n\nBuild: Choose the few strategic objectives to deploy in this cycle. Connect annual priorities to initiatives and observable measures. Assign ownership and discuss alignment through the organization's review process.\n\nCheck: Each initiative supports a stated priority. Measures assess the intended results rather than only activity volume. Relationship marks have an explicit and consistently applied meaning.\n\nPitfalls: Marking nearly every intersection and hiding actual choices. Treating a filled matrix as a substitute for iterative alignment discussion.\n\nExample: A maintenance organization connects reliability objectives to preventive-work priorities, specific improvement initiatives, outcome measures and named ownership roles.\n\nAlternatives: Strategy map, RACI matrix."
